  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "EFF argues that courts and legislatures should require police to obtain a warrant before searching stored ALPR data or adding plates to hotlists. The post catalogs documented abuses \u2014 including San Jose's 261,711 searches, Romani profiling, protest surveillance, an abortion-related search, ICE-related queries by San Francisco, retaliation against a Waukesha County critic, and officer stalking cases \u2014 to argue a probable-cause check would prevent them.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Where these systems still operate, courts and state legislatures must reduce their harms by (among other things) requiring police to get a warrant before searching stored ALPR data.",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "The first problem is the sheer number of times that SJPD officers search stored ALPR data: 261,711 times between June 5, 2024, and June 17, 2025, about 692 searches a day.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "San Francisco police let out-of-state agencies search the city's ALPR data (which itself violates California law), and at least 19 of those searches were marked as related to ICE",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    },
    {
      "quote": "officers reportedly ran more than 100 Flock searches against Jones, including some run by the deputy he had complained about.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    }
  ],
